Chemokines and some chemical analogs of chemokines prevent cellular HIV-1 entry when bound to the HIV-1 coreceptors C-C chemokine receptor 5 (CCR5) or C-X-C chemokine receptor 4 (CXCR4), which are G protein–­ coupled receptors (GPCRs). The ideal HIV-1 entry blocker targeting the coreceptors would display ligand bias and avoid activating G protein–mediated pathways that lead to inflammation. We compared CCR5-dependent activation of second messenger pathways in a single cell line. We studied two endogenous chemokines [RANTES (also known as CCL5) and MIP- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{1}\mathbf{a}$ (also known as CCL3)] and four chemokine analogs of RANTES (5P12-, 5P14-, 6P4-, and PSC-RANTES). We found that CCR5 signaled through both $\mathsf{\mathbf{G}_{i/o}}$ and $\mathsf{G}_{\mathsf{q}/11}.\mathsf{I P}_{1}$ accumulation and $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ flux arose from $\mathsf{G}_{\mathsf{q}/11}$ activation, rather than from $\mathsf{\pmb{G}}\mathsf{\pmb{\beta}}\mathsf{\pmb{\gamma}}$ subunit release after $\mathsf{G}_{\mathsf{i}/\mathsf{o}}$ activation as had been previously proposed. The 6P4- and PSC-RANTES analogs were superagonists for $\mathsf{G}_{\mathsf{q}/11}$ activation, whereas the 5P12- and 5P14-RANTES analogs displayed a signaling bias for $\mathsf{G}_{\mathsf{i}/\mathsf{o}}$ . These results demonstrate that RANTES analogs elicit G protein subtype–­ specific signaling bias and can cause CCR5 to couple preferentially to $\mathsf{G}_{\mathsf{q}/11}$ rather than to $\mathsf{G}_{\mathsf{i}/\mathsf{o}}$ signaling pathways. We propose that G protein subtype–specific signaling bias may be a general feature of GPCRs that can couple to more than one G protein family.
## INTRODUCTION
G protein–coupled receptors (GPCRs) can transmit extracellular cues from many structurally distinct ligands. GPCRs have been envisioned as on-off switches that become activated through the binding of an agonist ligand, which, in turn, can couple to and activate a single G protein class $(\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}},\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}/11}$ , $\mathrm{G}_{s/\mathrm{olf}}\mathrm{,}$ or ${\mathrm{G}}_{12/13}{\mathrm{;}}$ ). The G protein is a heterotrimeric protein consisting of a G subunit in complex with $\mathrm{G}\upbeta\upgamma$ subunits, which initially engages with the active GPCR and then dissociates into constituent $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{a}$ and G components. Depending on the particular G protein and its cellular context, either $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{\upalpha}$ or $\mathbf{G}\upbeta\upgamma$ can mediate downstream effector activity through activation or inhibition of enzymes or channels. The active receptor is desensitized through phosphorylation, which, in turn, mediates $\upbeta$ -arrestin recruitment. $\upbeta$ -Arrestin can facilitate receptor sequestration or internalization in addition to activating noncanonical signaling systems such as mitogen-activated protein kinase pathways. Canonically, GPCR signaling involves sequential activation of a single type of $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{\upalpha}$ protein class followed by $\upbeta$ -arrestin–dependent activation of noncanonical signal pathways. According to the concept of functional selectivity or ligand bias, signaling intensity can be skewed toward either the G protein pathway or the $\upbeta$ -arrestin pathway $(\boldsymbol{{l}},\boldsymbol{{2}})$ . Biased ligands are potentially desirable therapeutic agents that modulate specific signaling pathways relevant to disease processes (3). However, designing biased ligands requires a thorough understanding of receptor pharmacology and the appropriate cell-based assay systems to characterize the properties of drug candidates.
One disease target for biased ligands is HIV-1 infection, an on­ going epidemic with about 2.1 million new cases worldwide in 2015. A strategy to reduce transmission of HIV-1 is to prevent its cellular entry (4). C-C chemokine receptor 5 (CCR5) is an obligate coreceptor required for cellular viral entry. Cells lacking CCR5 on their surface are not susceptible to most strains of HIV-1 infection (5). Although endogenous chemokine agonists reduce the population of CCR5 on the cell surface by inducing receptor internalization, CCR5 agonists are not used as therapeutic agents for two reasons. First, they cause internalization of only a subset of CCR5, allowing HIV-1 to use receptors remaining on the cell surface for cellular entry (6). Second, stimulation of G proteins by activated CCR5 also promotes targeted migration of immune cells, leading to undesirable inflammatory side effects and enhanced HIV-1 infection at the site of inflammation (7, 8). In principle, a biased ligand that causes maximal receptor internalization, in the absence of G protein activation to avoid generating an inflammatory response, would be an ideal HIV-1 cellular entry blocker (9).
The desire for a biased, therapeutically viable HIV-1 drug that targets CCR5 has motivated the development of several analogs of regulated on activation, normal T cell expressed and secreted (RANTES) with N-terminal modifications (Fig. 1A): PSC-, 6P4-, 5P12-, and 5P14- RANTES (PSC, 6P4, 5P12, and 5P14 hereafter). These RANTES analogs appear to show pronounced signaling bias. Optimization of AOP-­RANTES, the first potent, nonsignaling chemokine analog that blocks HIV-1 entry (10), has generated PSC, the first highly potent anti– HIV-1 molecule targeting CCR5 to be developed (11). PSC apparently behaves as a pharmacological superagonist because it appears to be more efficacious than RANTES at eliciting the release of intracellular ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ stores and is more effective at causing internalization of CCR5 (9, 12). Recombinant RANTES analogs including 6P4, 5P12, and 5P14 have high anti–HIV-1 potency. Like PSC, 6P4 behaves as a strong agonist that induces both ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux and receptor internalization. In contrast, 5P14 does not cause ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux signaling but induces receptor internalization, suggestive of an internalization or $\upbeta$ -arrestin–biased ligand. Last, 5P12 binds to CCR5 but does not demonstrate apparent ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux or receptor internalization (9).
These RANTES analogs show promise in being capable of tuning the CCR5 signaling pathways to avoid the undesired side effect of inflammation. However, the signaling pathways that link CCR5 activation to its numerous physiological functions, including inflammation, remain poorly characterized. In addition to inducing ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux, activation of G proteins through CCR5 also leads to inhibition of cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) generation, an effect of $\mathrm{G_{i/o}}$ protein activation. However, it is less clear whether ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux is a result of $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ protein activation or release of $\operatorname{G}\beta\gamma$ subunits after $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ protein activation in the context of CCR5 (13). 5P14, which does not trigger CCR5-mediated ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux, does inhibit cAMP production (14). These results suggest that CCR5-mediated ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux and inhibition of cAMP generation are not linked and may be mediated by different G proteins. Some GPCRs can signal through more than one $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{\upalpha}$ protein class depending on the cellular environment and the activating ligand (15). CCR5 has been suggested to switch from $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ to $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ signaling, but the relationship between the well-established $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ signaling pathway and other G protein signaling pathways remains to be determined (16).
Here, we tested the ability of endogenous chemokines and RANTES analogs to activate different $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{\upalpha}$ protein classes in the same human cell line in culture. It was important to use the same cell line because compositional differences of effectors among cell types can make it possible for a ligand to appear to be biased when different pathways are evaluated in different cells, a bias that is not detected if the same cell types are used for all of the assays (17). We measured G protein activation through several downstream pathways, including inhibition of cAMP generation, ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux, and inositol 1-monophosphate $(\mathrm{IP}_{1})$ accumulation. In addition, we identified the activated G protein subtype that was responsible for these downstream events by performing assays in the presence of transfected individual G proteins or in cells treated with pertussis toxin (PTX) to inactivate $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ or an inhibitor (YM-254890) to block activation of $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ (18, 19). We found that each of the chemokines induced $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ protein activation, but the chemokines varied in their ability to activate $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ proteins. In comparison with the endogenous chemokines RANTES (also known as CCL5) and macrophage inflammatory protein- $1\upalpha$ (MIP-1) (also known as CCL3), 6P4 and PSC acted as superagonists at $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}},$ whereas 5P12 showed no $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ activation and was $\mathrm{G_{i/o}}$ biased.
RESULTS RANTES analogs induce CCR5-mediated inhibition of cAMP production, which depends on $\mathsf{\pmb{G}}_{\mathsf{i}/\mathsf{o}}$ protein activation
We first measured the ability of the RANTES analogs to induce $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ activation, which is the canonical signaling pathway of CCR5. $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ activation leads to the inhibition of adenylyl cyclase and a decrease in the amount of cellular cAMP. We tested the ligand-dependent inhibition of forskolin-stimulated cAMP production in cells transfected with CCR5 vector. Each ligand displayed a robust dose-dependent increase in inhibition of cAMP production (Fig. 1B). The $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ (the concentration of ligand required for half-maximal signal) values for inhibition of forskolin-stimulated cAMP accumulation determined for the ligands were all within 20-fold of each other, whereas the $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ (maximum signal) values were within about a 2-fold range (Table 1). At the highest concentration of ligand used $(100\mathrm{nM})$ , we found that the range of $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ values was 14 to $31\%$ inhibition of the forskolin response. The rank order of maximum signal was $5\mathrm{P}14>\mathrm{RANTES}>\mathrm{MIP-}1\upalpha=$ $6\mathrm{P}4=5\mathrm{P}12>\mathrm{PSC}$ . The $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ values of ligands ranged from 0.1 to $2.1\mathrm{nM}$ with the following rank order: $6\mathrm{P4}>5\mathrm{P14}>\mathrm{RANTES}=\mathrm{PSC}>\mathrm{MIP-1}\upalpha=$ 5P12 (Table 1). Signaling occurred specifically through CCR5, because empty vector–transfected cells did not show inhibition of cAMP generation in response to any of the chemokines tested (fig. S1). As a control for the effect of changes in CCR5 amount, experiments were also performed 48 hours after transfection (fig. S2), in addition to experiment performed 24 hours after transfection (cf. Fig. 1B).
To confirm that inhibition of cAMP accumulation was due to $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ protein activation, we measured cAMP accumulation in cells pretreated with PTX to prevent $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ proteins from interacting with receptors. Similar to the results from the dose-response experiment, each chemokine demonstrated statistically significant CCR5-mediated inhibition of cAMP accumulation in forskolin-treated cells. As expected, PTX pretreatment abrogated the ability of all ligands to inhibit forskolin-stimulated cAMP accumulation (Fig. 1C). 6P4 and PSC also induced significant enhancement of cAMP accumulation above forskolin-stimulated cells after treatment of PTX, as seen as negative percentage values of forskolin inhibition. However, 6P4 and PSC did not induce cAMP accumulation after treatment of PTX when forskolin was not added (fig. S3). We also present raw bioluminescence resonance energy transfer 2 $(\mathrm{BRET}^{2})$ ) values of the complete time course experiments (fig. S4, A to D).
## RANTES analogs have different effects on CCR5-dependent $\pmb{C a^{2+}}$ flux
In addition to inhibiting cAMP accumulation, CCR5 activation also leads to ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux. In contrast to inhibiting cAMP accumulation, not all of the ligands stimulated ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux through CCR5 (Fig. 2A). The RANTES analogs induced varying amounts of ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux with the rank order being $6\mathrm{P}4=\mathrm{PSC}>\mathrm{RANTES}>\mathrm{MIP}\mathrm{-}1\upalpha>5\mathrm{P}14>$ 5P12. Signaling occurred specifically through CCR5, because empty vector–transfected cells did not show ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ fluxes in response to any of the chemokines tested (fig. S5). Thus, for $\mathrm{Ca}^{\dot{2}+}$ flux, 6P4 and PSC are superagonists that are more efficacious than the endogenous CCR5 chemokines RANTES and MIP- $.1\upalpha$ , whereas 5P14 acts as a weak partial agonist and 5P12 does not have agonist activity.
$\cot^{2+}$ flux mediated by CCR5 is inhibited by $\pmb{G}_{12}$ cotransfection and increased by $\sigma_{\mathfrak{q}}$ or $\mathbf{G_{qi5}}$ cotransfection
Because of the differences in signaling profiles of the RANTES analogs between $\breve{\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux and inhibition of cAMP accumulation, we next investigated the G protein subtype that was responsible for eliciting $\mathrm{\dot{C}a}^{2+}$ flux through CCR5 activation. Classically, $\mathrm{\dot{C}a}^{2+}$ flux is a result of $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ protein activation, but chemokine receptors can also induce ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux through the release of $\mathbf{G}\upbeta\upgamma$ subunits after $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ protein activation (20, 21). To investigate the individual contributions of each G protein subtype to the ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux signal, we repeated the ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux experiments in cells in which CCR5 was cotransfected with $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}},\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}2},$ or $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{qi}5}$ protein. $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{qi5}}$ is an engineered $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ protein containing the last five amino acid residues of $G_{\mathrm{i}2}$ , which allows $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ -coupled GPCRs to signal through $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ downstream signaling pathways (22). Cotransfection of CCR5 with the three G protein subunits did not affect the amount of CCR5 on the cell surface (fig. S6). Also, ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux in response to adenosine $5^{\prime}$ -­triphosphate (ATP) in HEK293T cells was not affected by cotransfection of G protein subunits (fig. S7). ATP was used as a positive control to activate endogenous purinergic receptors in HEK293T cells.
Cotransfection of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{G}}_{12}$ with CCR5 markedly inhibited the ability of RANTES and MIP- $1\upalpha$ to induce ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux through CCR5 (Fig. 2B). $\mathrm{G}_{12}$ cotransfection also dampened the maximum $\mathrm{\dot{C}a}^{2+}$ flux signal elicited in response to PSC. In contrast, cotransfection of CCR5 and $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ enhanced the maximal ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux signal for all ligands, except 5P12 (Fig. 2C). In cells cotransfected with CCR5 and a promiscuous G protein construct, $\mathrm{G}_{\P^{15}}$ , each RANTES analog induced robust ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux (Fig. 2D), confirming the ability of all the RANTES analogs to activate $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ through CCR5. The data were replotted to highlight differences in signal caused by cotransfection of CCR5 with $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}},\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}2}$ , or $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{qi}5}$ (fig. S8A).
Because of differences in the binding kinetics of ligands, measurements of ligand bias are sensitive to the experimental time scale (23, 24). For example, ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux assays measure a transient signal that is complete in $2\mathrm{min}$ , whereas cAMP accumulation is measured over durations of $15\mathrm{min}$ or more. To accurately compare the ability of each chemokine to activate CCR5-mediated $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ flux and inhibition of cAMP accumulation, it was necessary to measure ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux with an accumulation assay. Inositol 1,4,5-trisphosphate $(\mathrm{IP}_{3})$ is a pro­duct upstream of ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux, and a degradation product of $\mathrm{IP}_{3}$ can be measured by an accumulation assay. Although $\mathrm{IP}_{3}$ is rapidly degraded, its degradation product $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ is stabilized by the addition of lithium chloride, allowing it to accumulate in the cell after receptor stimulation. $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation above that induced by buffer was not seen in vector-transfected negative control cells treated with any ligand (fig. S9). In CCR5-expressing cells, we observed $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation in response to only PSC (Fig. 2E), demonstrating that PSC functions as a superagonist as measured by $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation and ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux.
Cotransfection of $\mathrm{{G}}_{12}$ with CCR5 reduced $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation in response to PSC (Fig. 2F). In contrast, $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ cotransfection with CCR5 enhanced $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation in response to each of the chemokines, except 5P12 (Fig. 2G). Moreover, $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ cotransfection with CCR5 revealed agonist activity as measured by $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation in response to 6P4, RANTES, MIP- ${1\mathrm{a}},$ , and 5P14, an effect not seen in cells transfected with only CCR5. In addition, the rank order of maximal $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation signal among the RANTES analogs was similar to that shown for ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux, with $6{\mathrm{P}}4={\mathrm{PSC}}>{\mathrm{RANTES}}>5{\mathrm{P}}14>5{\mathrm{P}}12.$ Moreover, each of the RANTES analogs showed robust $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation in cells cotransfected with $\mathrm{G}_{\P^{15}}$ , confirming that each ligand activates $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ (Fig. 2H). The data were replotted to highlight differences in signal caused by cotransfection of CCR5 with $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}},\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}2}.$ , or $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{qi}5}$ (fig. S8B).
Together, our $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation and ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux data show that PSC and 6P4 are superagonists, whereas 5P12 lacks agonist activity in these assays. Furthermore, $\mathrm{\bfG}_{12}$ cotransfection disrupts the ability of both endogenous CCR5 chemokines and RANTES analogs to induce ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux and $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation, whereas $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ cotransfection has the opposite effect.
CCR5-mediated $\cot^{2+}$ flux is reduced by inhibiting $\pmb{6}_{\mathbf{i}/\mathbf{o}}$ protein activation and abolished by inhibiting $\mathsf{\pmb{G}}_{\mathsf{\pmb{q}}}$ protein activation
To further differentiate between the contribution of $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ and $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ proteins to CCR5-mediated signaling, we treated cells with G protein subtype–specific inhibitors. PTX prevents $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ protein coupling to GPCRs by catalyzing the adenosine $5^{\prime}$ -diphosphate ribosylation of a Cys residue in the C-terminal tail of $\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}\mathbf{\Lambda}\mathbf{\vec{a}}$ subunits (25). YM254890 prevents $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ activation by acting as a guanine-nucleotide dissociation inhibitor of $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ (26). Both PTX and YM-254890 decreased CCR5-mediated $\dot{\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux (Fig.  3, A to D). However, YM-254890 abolished CCR5-mediated $\check{\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux stimulated by all chemokines, whereas the effects of PTX were less pronounced. In CCR5-expressing cells, PTX reduced ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux induced by the superagonists 6P4 and PSC and completely inhibited that induced by RANTES and MIP- $.1\upalpha$ (Fig. 3A). In cells cotransfected with CCR5 and $\mathrm{G}_{12}$ , both PTX and YM-254890 decreased the remaining signal from PSC and 6P4 (Fig. 3B). However, in cells cotransfected with CCR5 and ${\bf G}_{\bf q},$ which would be expected to increase CCR5 signaling, RANTES, MIP- $1\upalpha$ , and 5P14 triggered a partially PTX-insensitive ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux response (Fig. 3C). 6P4 and PSC also showed more PTX insensitivity in cells cotransfected with $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ and CCR5 than in cells expressing CCR5 alone. Last, the signal elicited by chemokines in cells cotransfected with CCR5 and the $\mathrm{G}_{\P^{i5}}$ chimera was reduced as a result of PTX treatment, except for that triggered by 5P12 (Fig. 3D). Overall, in contrast to PTX, disruption of $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ activation by YM-254890 abolished ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ signaling by each ligand even when $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ or $\mathrm{G}_{\P^{15}}$ was cotransfected.
CCR5-mediated $\|\mathsf{P}_{1}$ accumulation is reduced by inhibiting $\pmb{6}_{\mathbf{i}/\mathbf{o}}$ protein activation and abolished by inhibiting $\mathsf{\pmb{G_{q}}}$
Consistent with the ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux experiments, YM-254890 prevented all of the chemokines from causing $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation, regardless of the $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{\upalpha}$ protein subunit cotransfected (Fig. 4, A to D). In cells transfected with CCR5 alone, the PSC-induced $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ signal was attenuated by PTX (Fig. 4A). PTX treatment of cells cotransfected with CCR5 and $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ only minimally reduced the $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ signal induced by RANTES, MIP-1, 6P4, and PSC, but had a greater effect on that induced by 5P14 (Fig. 4C). As with ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux, PTX did not strongly reduce the signal elicited by the RANTES analogs when CCR5 was cotransfected with $\mathrm{G}_{\P^{i5}}$ (Fig. 4D). In summary, although PTX caused only a modest decrease in chemokine responses, YM-254890 abolished the ability of each of the ligands tested to elicit $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation and ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux. Thus, both cotransfection and inhibition experiments suggest that $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ is the dominant G protein in stimulating ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux after CCR5 activation, with $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ playing a much smaller role.
Cotransfection of CCR5 with $\bf{G}_{i/o_{i}}$ , ${\sf G}_{{\sf q}},$ or $\mathbf{G}_{\mathbf{q}\mathbf{i}5}$ causes different effects on ${\pmb E}_{\mathbf{max}}$ values and $\tt E C_{50}$ values
The effects of G protein cotransfection with CCR5 could be a result of a change in ligand $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ or $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ . Performing and fitting doseresponse curves for ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux (Table 2) showed that in cells transfected with CCR5, $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ values ranged from 21.0 to $240\mathrm{nM}$ with the rank order of ${\mathrm{PSC}}=6{\mathrm{P4}}={\mathrm{RANTES}}=5{\mathrm{P14}}>{\mathrm{MIP-1\alpha}}$ (Fig. 5A). $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ values ranged from 2700 to 45,000 RFU, and the rank order was $6\mathrm{P}4>\mathrm{PSC}>\mathrm{RANTES}=\mathrm{MIP}-\mathrm{1}\alpha>5\mathrm{P}14.$ Similar results were obtained 48 hours after transfection (fig. S10). Cotransfection of CCR5 and $\mathrm{G}_{12}$ decreased ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux such that only PSC and 6P4 could elicit any ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux response (Fig. 5B), which was due to reduced $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ values because $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ values in cells transfected with CCR5 were similar to those in cells cotransfected with both CCR5 and $G_{\mathrm{i}2}$ . Similarly, the increase in response from each chemokine in cells cotransfected with CCR5 and $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ was not a result of altered $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ values, but rather due to an increase in $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ values (Fig. 5C). In contrast to $\ensuremath{\mathrm{G}}_{12}$ and $\mathrm{G_{q}}.$ , cotransfection of $\mathrm{G}_{\mathtt{q i}5}$ and CCR5 altered both $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ and $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ values in dose-response ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux experiments (Fig. 5D). The range of $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ values among the ligands tested decreased, but the rank order was similar, with $6\mathrm{P}4=\mathrm{RANTES}>\mathrm{PSC}>5\mathrm{P}14=\mathrm{MIP}{-}1\upalpha>$ 5P12. In addition, the $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ value of each ligand increased, but the rank order also remained the same.
In cells transfected with CCR5 alone, only PSC showed any dosedependent $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation, with an $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ of $190~\mathrm{nM}$ (Fig.  5E) (Table 3). No agonist-stimulated $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation was observed in cells cotransfected with CCR5 and $G_{\mathrm{i}2}$ (Fig. 5F). Cells cotransfected with CCR5 and $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ displayed a range of $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ values for $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation of 4.3 to $72{\mathrm{nM}}$ with a rank order of $5\mathrm{P}14>6\mathrm{P}4>\mathrm{RANTES}=$ PSC. These $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ values were similar to those for ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux (Fig. 5, C and G). The $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ values ranged from 1.0 to 8.4 pmol per well with a rank order of $\mathrm{PSC}>6\mathrm{P4}>5\mathrm{P}14>\mathrm{RANTES},$ although the curve fits for 5P12 and MIP-1 were somewhat ambiguous. This rank order of $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ values was similar to that observed with ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux in CCR5- transfected cells both with and without cotransfection of $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ . As seen previously, cotransfection of $\mathrm{G}_{\P^{15}}$ and CCR5 allowed each RANTES analog to cause $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation (Fig. 5H). This increase in signal caused by $\mathrm{G}_{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{qi}}5}$ cotransfection with CCR5 could be attributed to a decrease in $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ values for all ligands tested. In addition, an increase in $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ contributed to the higher signals for RANTES, MIP1, 5P12, and 5P14 in cells cotransfected with $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{qi}5}$ and CCR5.
In summary, $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ values were altered when $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ or $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ was cotransfected with CCR5, whereas both $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ and $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ values were altered when $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{qi}5}$ was cotransfected with CCR5. These results show that when CCR5 is cotransfected with either $G_{\mathrm{i}2}$ or ${\bf G}_{\bf q},$ the $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ values, but not the $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ values, for agonist-dependent $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation and ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux were altered. In contrast, cotransfection of CCR5 and $\mathrm{G}_{\P^{\ i5}}$ caused a decrease in $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ values for $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation and ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux for all ligands tested. In addition, coexpression of CCR5 and $\mathrm{G}_{\P^{15}}$ led to an increase in $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ values for ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux for all ligands and an increase in $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ values for $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation for most ligands tested.
## DISCUSSION
Several GPCR-targeted ligands have been described to cause signaling bias toward $\upbeta$ -arrestin or $G$ protein signaling pathways. However, reports of ligands that bias GPCR signaling toward a specific $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{\upalpha}$ protein class are rare, in part, because of the difficulty in interpreting signaling assays in which cross-talk between different G protein signaling pathways occurs. In this study, we determined the specific G protein class that was responsible for CCR5-mediated inhibition of cAMP accumulation, ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux, and $\mathrm{IP}_{3}$ production. Analogs of the endogenous chemokine RANTES with N-terminal modifications showed similar abilities to activate CCR5-mediated $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ signaling, but either enhanced or decreased abilities to activate CCR5-mediated $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ signaling.
For inhibition of cAMP accumulation, each of the endogenous chemokines and the RANTES analogs tested acted as agonists with similar $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ values and $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ values of $2.1\mathrm{nM}$ or lower. $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ values of at least one order of magnitude higher were obtained earlier from CCR5-expressing Chinese hamster ovary (CHO-CCR5) cells, which also showed a lack of detectable signal for 5P12 (14). In addition to the use of different cells, the source of variation between these results could be due to the use of different ligand incubation times or different assays to detect cAMP [real-time changes of cAMP in live cells compared with measurement of cAMP after a single incubation time point (14)].
The RANTES analogs showed different abilities to induce ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux and $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation through CCR5. In comparison to the endogenous chemokines RANTES and MIP- ${1\mathrm{a}},$ , the RANTES analogs 6P4 and PSC acted as superagonists, whereas 5P12 did not have agonist activity in the ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux assays. Our measurements of $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ and $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ values recapitulated the results from ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux assays performed in CCR5-transfected HelaP5L and phytohemagglutinin- and interleukin2–activated T blasts (9). Cells expressing only CCR5 demonstrated $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation in response only to PSC, whereas CCR5-mediated $\dot{\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux was measured in response to RANTES, MIP-1, 6P4, and PSC. The differences between results when assessing ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux and $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation are likely due to assay sensitivity (27). The ability of the RANTES analogs to induce CCR5-mediated ${\bf G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ signaling appears to correlate with their ability to induce $\upbeta$ -arrestin recruitment and internalization. Analogous to the ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux results, only 5P12 was unable to induce CCR5 internalization (28). In addition, cells treated with PSC demonstrated the strongest recruitment of $\upbeta$ -arrestin to CCR5, followed by RANTES, and then 5P14. 5P12 did not cause $\upbeta$ -arrestin recruitment to CCR5 (14, 29, 30).
The differences in the signaling profiles of the RANTES analogs suggest that different effectors are responsible for CCR5-mediated inhibition of cAMP accumulation or induction of ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux. $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ protein activation inhibits cAMP accumulation, a pathway further confirmed by our results. 6P4 and PSC can stimulate cAMP production above levels induced by forskolin in the presence of PTX, suggesting that these ligands might also stimulate CCR5-mediated $G_{s}$ protein activation. However, 6P4 and PSC did not demonstrate CCR5-mediated cAMP accumulation in cells treated with PTX, but not with forskolin. The differential regulation of adenylated cyclase subtypes in HEK293 cells could also be the source of this PTXdependent cAMP production by 6P4 and PSC. ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ can activate certain subtypes of adenylate cyclase, causing cross-talk between ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ signaling and cAMP production (31). Alternatively, 6P4 and PSC in the presence of PTX could inhibit the degradation of forskolindependent cAMP, and these ligands would not induce cAMP production in the presence of PTX, but the absence of forskolin.
The $\mathbf{G}\mathbf{a}$ protein subunit that is responsible for ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux and $\mathrm{IP}_{3}$ formation is less clear, but could be $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ or $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ . Cotransfection of $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ augments CCR5-mediated ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux and $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation, responses that are inhibited by cotransfection of $\mathrm{G}_{12},$ which supports the hypothesis that $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ is responsible for ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux. $\mathrm{{G}}_{12}$ transfection could inhibit ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux and $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation through CCR5 either by preventing $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ recruitment by competitively binding to CCR5 or by acting as a $\mathrm{G}\upbeta\upgamma$ subunit scavenger, which would prevent the formation of heterotrimeric $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ proteins required for G protein activity.
We further differentiated the contributions of $\mathrm{G_{i/o}}$ and $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ proteins to CCR5-mediated ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux and $\mathrm{IP}_{3}$ signaling using inhibitors. The $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ inhibitor YM-254890 prevented all of the RANTES analogs from inducing CCR5-mediated ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux and $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation. However, the ability of PTX to at least partially inhibit ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux and $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation does not support the hypothesis that $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ mediates these signaling pathways through CCR5. This seemingly paradoxical result can be explained by the indirect effect of PTX decreasing binding of chemokines to CCR5 (6). Reduction of agonist affinity is likely due to a reduction of $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ protein precoupling to CCR5. Structural studies of $\upbeta_{2}$ adrenergic receptor interacting with G protein has confirmed the paradigm that a receptor must be bound to a transducer to stabilize the agonist-receptor interaction (32). Likewise, the conformation of CCR5 molecules on the cellular surface is altered upon PTX-induced abrogation of $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ precoupling (33). Similar to our results, other reports have also described incomplete inhibition of CCR5-mediated ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux by PTX, suggesting that $\mathrm{G_{i/o}}$ plays a role in agonist binding but not in ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux (34–36). Last, it is unlikely that $\mathbf{G}\upbeta\upgamma$ subunits could induce ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux in HEK293T cells, because the closely related HEK293 cell line does not produce the phospholipase C subunit that links $\mathbf{G}\upbeta\upgamma$ release to $\mathrm{Ca}^{\dot{2}+}$ flux (37). Although we cannot rule out a contribution from $\mathrm{G}\upbeta\upgamma$ , our data suggest that ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux signaling through CCR5 in this cellular system is mainly, if not completely, mediated by $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ protein activation.
Chemokine receptors are historically believed to couple to and signal through $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ proteins exclusively. The ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux response elicited by many chemokine receptors has been thought to be a result of $\mathrm{G}\upbeta\upgamma$ subunit release after activation of $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ (20). However, we provide evidence that CCR5 signals through both $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ and $\mathbf{G}_{\mathbf{q}/11}$ proteins. We also demonstrate that $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ accumulation and ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux are products of $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ protein signaling in HEK293T cells. CCR5 mediates PTX-insensitive signaling responses, suggesting that CCR5 couples to and signals through other G proteins (16, 34, 35). CCR5 couples to and coimmunoprecipitates with $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ (38, 39). Both ${\bf G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ and $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ protein signaling may contribute to the activities of other chemo­ kine receptors. CXCR4 is proposed to switch from $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ signaling to $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ signaling, leading to reduced migration and activation of the T cells (16, 40). In addition, CXCR4- and CCR7-dependent chemotaxis of dendritic cells requires activation of both $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ and $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ pathways (41). Theoretically, the capacity of a receptor to couple to certain G protein subunits should be apparent from its primary structure. However, although residues in similar positions in different GPCRs recognize G proteins, other distinct residues make structural binding contacts. Therefore, without the availability of comparable structures of $\mathrm{G_{i/o}}$ and $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ bound to activated GPCRs, it is difficult to surmise whether $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ coupling to other chemokine receptors can be predicted or validated by structural information (42).
receptors not precoupled to $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ protein (6, 43). CCR5 can assume multiple conformations, as shown by monoclonal antibody detection of different populations of CCR5 (44). The endogenous chemokines RANTES and MIP- $1\upalpha$ bind with high affinity to only $\mathrm{G_{i/o}}$ protein– coupled CCR5, whereas PSC has a high affinity for both $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ protein– coupled CCR5 and naked CCR5 (6). Single-molecule binding experiments have also shown that 5P12, 5P14, PSC, and 6P4 bind to
CCR5 with affinities of $374\pm24\mathrm{pM}$ , $494\pm38\mathrm{pM}$ , $\mathrm{10.1\pm0.4pM}$ , and $6.6\pm0.3\mathrm{pM}$ , respectively, in detergent solution and in absence of G protein. However, MIP-1 and RANTES do not show detectable binding to naked receptor up to concentrations of $10\upmu\mathrm{M}$ under these conditions (45). Thus, PSC can activate a subset of CCR5 molecules that remains inactive after stimulation with RANTES (43). Therefore, the increased ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux in response to PSC or 6P4 is likely due to the activation of naked CCR5, in addition to $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ protein–precoupled CCR5. In line with this hypothesis, PSC and 6P4 do not have substantially lower $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ values than RANTES, but do have higher $E_{\mathrm{max}}$ values, suggesting that these chemokines are superagonists because they bind to more receptors, rather than because they bind with higher affinity.
We present a model to contextualize the results of our experiments and hypothesize about how RANTES analogs are capable of showing G protein subtype–specific signaling bias (Fig. 6A). For RANTES and MIP-1, $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ subunit precoupling promotes binding of the ligand to the receptor, upon which $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ is activated and dissociates from the receptor. A ligand that remains bound to the receptor can recruit and activate $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ or additional $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ proteins. In contrast to RANTES and MIP- $1\upalpha$ , PSC and 6P4 can bind to and activate CCR5-dependent signaling pathways, independently of whether the receptor is precoupled to $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ or naked. By signaling through two pools of the receptor, PSC and 6P4 can produce more signals and thus act as superagonists. Although not apparent from our data, 5P12 binds to CCR5 in a G protein–independent fashion (6), and thus, binding of 5P12 to naked CCR5 preferentially activates $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ (Fig. 6B).
This study demonstrates a type of ligand bias that is G protein subtype specific. Another peptide receptor, pituitary adenylate cyclase activating polypeptide (PACAP) receptor, responds to the two ligands PACAP-38 and PACAP-27 with similar $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ values for the $G_{s}$ signaling pathway. However, the two PACAP receptor ligands have $\mathrm{EC}_{50}$ values that are 100-fold different for a $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ signaling pathway (46). The free fatty acid receptors (FFARs) also signal through multiple G protein subtypes, but depending on the activating ligand, the FFARs can selectively signal through a single G protein subtype. For example, synthetic agonists targeting FFAR1 can signal through both $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ and ${\bf G}_{s},$ whereas the endogenous agonists signal only through $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ (47). In addition, an allosteric modulator targeting FFAR2 selectively activates $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ signaling but not $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ signaling (48). In our study, we described chemokines with similar capacity to evoke CCR5- mediated $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ signaling, but with both diminished and enhanced $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ signaling.
How the modification of the N-terminal region of RANTES leads to such pronounced changes in CCR5-mediated $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ signaling, but not $\mathrm{G_{i/o}}$ signaling, is unclear. Nonetheless, some insight can be garnered from crystal structures of CCR5, including CCR5 in complex with 5P7, another RANTES analog (49, 50). 5P7 differs from 5P12 by one amino acid, Thr instead of Leu at position 7, and lacks agonist activity for ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux (9). When comparing the crystal structure of 5P7 to a model of CCR5 with RANTES, several different interactions are apparent that could be key for $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ protein activation. Whereas RANTES has polar residues in positions 4 to 7 that can engage a minor pocket polar network, 5P7 has bulky hydrophobic residues at these positions. Similarly, the $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ -inactive RANTES analogs 5P12 and 5P14 have bulky hydrophobic residues at positions 4 to 7, whereas the superagonist PSC has polar residues. However, the superagonist 6P4 has mostly less-bulky hydrophobic amino acids at these positions. A crystal structure of CCR5 engaged with a
Lorenzen et al., Sci. Signal. 11, eaao6152 (2018)     16 October 2018 superagonist will be necessary to understand precisely how $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ signaling is modulated by changes in the N-terminal sequences of RANTES.
The RANTES analogs 5P12 and 5P14, which fail to activate ${\bf G}_{\mathrm{q}},$ may be safer drugs because they discriminate between $\mathrm{G_{i/o}}$ and ${\bf G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ protein activation. In bypassing $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ signaling, several physiological effects of CCR5 activation that are PTX resistant or downstream of ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux would be avoided, including CCR5 recruitment to the immunological synapse during T cell stimulation (16). In addition, an in vitro predictor of an inflammatory response, chemotaxis, requires additional GPCR signaling because $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ signaling is necessary, but not sufficient, to induce chemotaxis (21). Because ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux is necessary for actin rearrangement, an important step in chemotaxis, it is plausible that this additional signaling component is $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ activation (51). Although the RANTES chemokine analogs have not been compared in their ability to induce chemotaxis, PSC does cause chemotaxis of CD4 T cells (6). The contribution of these two signaling arms to the physiological effects of CCR5 activation remains unknown. Thus, these RANTES analogs could serve as tools to determine the effects of $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ signaling alone compared with $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ and $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ protein signaling in combination. Future studies should use these chemokine analogs to determine how CCR5- mediated $\mathrm{G_{i/o}}$ and $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ signaling affect events such as chemotaxis and T cell activation using physiologically relevant cell types.
In conclusion, we investigated G protein subtype–specific biased signaling of the chemokine receptor CCR5 using a series of chemokine analogs developed as HIV-1 microbicides. Using a specific inhibitor, we found that $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}/11}$ is responsible for ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ flux downstream of CCR5 in HEK293T cells, which earlier had been attributed to $\mathbf{G}\upbeta\upgamma$ subunit released from $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ . RANTES analogs had a wide range of efficacy for CCR5-mediated $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ signaling, but similar $\mathrm{G_{i/o}}$ signaling efficacy, opening the possibility for selectively tuning chemo­ kine signaling. Noncanonical $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}/11}$ signaling bias in the chemokine receptor network to our knowledge has not been previously reported. Our findings also provide an experimental strategy in which a ${\bf G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ -specific inhibitor is used to study $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ signaling bias relevant to $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ flux signals from GPCRs thought to couple to $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}/\mathrm{o}}$ .
## MATERIALS AND METHODS Materials
Coelenterazine 400A for BRET2 experiments was from Biotium. The IP-One HTRF assay kit was from Cisbio. Forskolin, PTX, and poly-d-lysine were from Sigma-Aldrich. YM-254890 was from Wako Pure Chemical Industries. Dulbecco’s modified Eagle’s medium Glutamax (DMEM-Q), Hanks’ balanced salt solution (HBSS), and Lipofectamine 2000 were from Life Technologies. Fetal bovine serum (FBS) was from Atlanta Biologicals. Bovine serum albumin (BSA) fraction V, fatty acid free was from Roche, and 96-well white, clear bottom microplates and 384-well black, clear bottom microplates were from Corning. RANTES and MIP-1 were from PeproTech. The RANTES analogs 5P12, 5P14, 6P4, and PSC were a gift from O. Hartley (Université de Genève).
## Transfection constructs
The human CCR5 complementary DNA (cDNA) in pcDNA3.1(+) encoded a C-terminal 1D4 epitope tag (TETSQVAPA). The exchange protein directly activated by cAMP 2 (EPAC2) reporter was a gift from M. Bouvier (Université de Montréal). $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ and $\mathbf{G}_{12}\mathbf{a}$ subunit plasmids are wild-type human cDNA cloned in pcDNA $3.1(+)$ (cDNA Resource Center). $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{qi}5}$ refers to a construct in which the $\upalpha$ subunit of $\mathrm{G_{q}}$ is modified so that its C-terminal tail amino acid residues are derived from the $\upalpha$ subunit of ${\bf G}_{12}$ (such that EYNLV-COOH in $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ becomes DCGLFCOOH in $\mathrm{G}_{\P^{\bf{i}}}\mathrm{}_{5}^{\cdot}$ ). $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{qi}5}$ was cloned into pcDNA1 (Addgene) (22).
## Cell culture and transfection
HEK293T cells were maintained in DMEM-Q with $10\%$ FBS. Transient transfections were performed using Lipofectamine 2000 according to the manufacturer’s instructions with some modifications as previously described (52). Cells were transfected in different plate formats with the following amounts of total DNA: $2~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ per well in 6-well plates, $100\mathrm{ng}$ per well in 96-well plates, and $20\mathrm{ng}$ per well in 384-well plates. Total transfected plasmid DNA was kept constant by adding empty vector pcDNA3.1 $\left(+\right)$ when necessary.
## Adenylyl cyclase activity assay
Inhibition of forskolin-stimulated cAMP production in response to each chemokine was monitored in HEK293T cells cotransfected with CCR5 and an EPAC reporter protein that shows a decrease in BRET upon cAMP binding (53). For each well of a 96-well plate, HEK293T cells (100,000 in $0.1~\mathrm{ml}$ of DMEM) were transfected with $12~\mathrm{ng}$ of RLuc3-EPAC-GFP (green fluorescent protein), a ${\mathrm{BRET}}^{2}$ cAMP sensor, with or without cotransfection of $24~\mathrm{ng}$ of CCR5 wild type. Cells were then plated at $100\upmu\mathrm{l}$ per well into 96-well white, clear bottom microplates previously coated with $0.01\%$ (w/v) poly-d-lysine. Twenty-four hours after transfection, medium was replaced with BRET buffer [phosphate-buffered saline (PBS) containing $0.5~\mathrm{mM}$ $\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}$ and $0.1\%$ BSA]. Coelenterazine 400A was added to a final concentration of $5\upmu\mathrm{M}$ followed by a 5-min incubation at room temperature. Cells were then stimulated with chemokine in the presence or absence of $5\upmu\mathrm{M}$ forskolin at room temperature for $5\mathrm{min}$ before $\mathrm{BRET}^{2}$ reading to yield dose-response curves. For kinetic studies, HEK293T cells were transfected as previously described with $15\mathrm{mM}$ Hepes added to the culture media. Coelenterazine 400A was added in a single column of the 96-well plate, and after $5\mathrm{min}$ , wells were treated with chemokine in the presence of $5\upmu\mathrm{M}$ forskolin or $200\upmu\mathrm{M}$ 3-isobutyl-1-methylxanthine to assess the inhibition of forskolinstimulated cAMP production or cAMP production, respectively. Then, the plate was immediately read repetitively for $12\mathrm{min}$ . Cells were treated and wells were read one single column of the 96-well plate at a time, producing one replicate at a time, and repeated six times per plate to produce six replicates per experiment. Luminescence and fluorescence readings were collected using the Synergy NEO2 plate reader (BioTek Instruments) and Gen5 software. BRET2 readings between RLuc3 and GFP10 were collected by sequential integration of the signals detected in the 365- to 435-nm (RLuc3) and 505- to ${525-}\mathrm{nm}$ (GFP10) windows. BRET2 ratios were calculated as previously described (52) and are shown as a percentage of the forskolin-­stimulated response. For experiments involving PTX, cells were treated with PTX $\left(100~\mathrm{{ng/ml}}\right)$ for 16 hours at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $5\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ before stimulation with forskolin and chemokines. All experimental manipulations were performed while cells remained attached to the 96-well plates.
## $\pmb{C a^{2+}}$ flux assay
${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ mobilization was monitored by loading CCR5-transfected HEK293T cells with a $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ -sensitive fluorescent dye and measuring changes in cytosolic ${\mathrm{Ca}}^{2+}$ concentrations immediately after chemokine addition. For each well of a 384-well plate, HEK293T cells (20,000 in $0.02{\mathrm{ml}}$ of DMEM) were transfected with $10~\mathrm{{ng}}$ of CCR5 or $20~\mathrm{{ng}}$ of empty vector. Where applicable, $5\mathrm{ng}$ of $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ , $\mathrm{G}_{12},$ or $\mathrm{G}_{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{qi}}5}$ vector constructs was cotransfected. Cells were then plated at $20\mathrm{\dot{\upmul}}$ per well into 384-well black, clear bottom microplates previously coated with $0.01\%$ (w/v) poly-d-lysine hydrobromide. Twenty-four hours after transfection, FLIPR Calcium 6 dye $20\upmu\mathrm{l}$ per well; Molecular Devices) was added to the cells and incubated for 1.5 hours at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with $5\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The dye was dissolved in HBSS-H [HBSS with $20~\mathrm{mM}$ Hepes $\mathrm{(pH7.4)}]$ ] with $0.4\%$ BSA. Before measurement, the plate was incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for an additional $30\mathrm{min}$ in a prewarmed FlexStation II 384 Plate Reader (Molecular Devices). Chemokine at a $5\times$ final concentration was diluted in HBSS-H with $0.2\%$ BSA. Fluorescence readings were collected using the FlexStation plate reader with excitation at $485~\mathrm{nm}$ , emission at $535~\mathrm{nm}$ , and the dichroic mirror at $525\mathrm{nm}$ . The FlexStation took measurements over a $100-s$ time course, with $10\upmu\mathrm{l}$ of ligand added to the cells $20s$ after the start of measurement. RFU are reported as the peak magnitude signal minus the basal signal in each well. For experiments involving PTX, cells were treated with PTX $(100~\mathrm{{ng/ml})}$ for 16 hours at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $5\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ before chemokine stimulation. For experiments involving
YM-254890, $10\upmu\mathrm{M}$ YM-254890 [dissolved as a $10\mathrm{mM}$ stock in dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO)] or equivalent DMSO vehicle was added to the cells and incubated for $30\mathrm{min}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## $\|\mathsf{P}_{1}$ accumulation assay
$\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ concentrations in CCR5-transfected cells were measured using a competitive homogeneous time-resolved fluorescence assay after incubation with each chemokine for 3 hours. For each well of a low-volume, 384-well plate, HEK293T cells $5000\mathrm{in}7\upmu\mathrm{l}$ of DMEM) were transfected with $11~\mathrm{{ng}}$ of CCR5 vector or $11~\mathrm{{ng}}$ of empty vector. Where applicable, $5.5~\mathrm{ng}$ of $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}},\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}2}.$ , or $\mathrm{G}_{\P^{i5}}$ vector constructs was cotransfected. Twenty-four hours after transfection, cells were stimulated by chemokine diluted in $7\upmu\mathrm{l}$ of $1\times$ stimulation buffer $\mathrm{\Delta}_{\cdot}10\mathrm{\mM}$ Hepes, 1 mM $\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$ , 0.5 mM $\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}$ , 4.2 mM KCl, $146~\mathrm{mM}$ NaCl, $5.5~\mathrm{mM}$ glucose, and $50~\mathrm{mM}$ LiCl $\mathrm{(pH7.4)}$ ] with $0.2\%$ BSA and $50~\mathrm{mM}$ LiCl (to prevent $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ degradation). The chemokine was incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 hours. After incubation, cells were lysed by the addition of d2-fluorophore–labeled $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ analog ${\it3\upmu\mathrm{l}}$ per well) as the fluorescence acceptor and the terbium cryptate–labeled anti- ${\bf\cdot I P}_{1}$ monoclonal antibody as the fluorescence donor. Both fluorescent donor and acceptor were diluted in the kit-supplied lysis buffer. The plates were incubated overnight at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and time-resolved fluorescence signals were read using the BioTek Synergy NEO plate reader (BioTek Instruments, Winooski, VT) at 620 and $655~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Results were calculated as a $665\mathrm{-nm}/620\mathrm{-nm}$ signal ratio, and $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ concentrations were interpolated from a standard curve prepared using the supplied $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ calibrator. Results are shown as picomoles of $\mathrm{IP}_{1}$ formed per well. For experiments involving PTX, cells were treated with PTX $\mathrm{{'}100~n g/m l)}$ for 16 hours at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $5\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ before stimulation. For experiments involving YM-254890, $10\upmu\mathrm{M}$ YM-254890 or equivalent DMSO vehicle was added to the cells and incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ .
## Measurements of cell surface CCR5
The amount of CCR5 on the cell surface was determined by flow cytometry. For one well of a six-well plate, HEK293T cells (1,000,000 in $2\mathrm{ml}$ of DMEM) were transfected with $2\upmu\up g$ of empty vector, $1\upmu\up g$ of CCR5 plus $1\upmu\up g$ of empty vector, or $1\upmu\up g$ of CCR5 plus $0.5~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ of $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{i}2},\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{q}}$ or $\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{qi}5}$ and $0.5\upmu\mathrm{g}$ of empty vector. Twenty-four hours after transfection, cells were detached in ice-cold PBS and transferred to a microcentrifuge tube. Cells were spun down and then incubated with $100~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ of fluorescence-activated cell sorting buffer (PBS with 0.5 mM $\mathrm{MgCl_{2}}+0.1\%$ BSA) containing ${8\upmu\mathrm{l}}$ of anti-CCR5 T21/8 phycoerythrin-conjugated antibody (eBioscience) for $60\mathrm{min}$ on ice. Antibody incubation was followed by three washes with ice-cold PBS. The cell surface receptor amount was quantified by flow cytometry using the Accuri C6 flow cytometer (BD Biosciences) within 1 hour after preparation.
## Data analysis
Data were graphed and analyzed using GraphPad Prism 6.0 software (San Diego, CA). Dose-response curves were fitted by a three-parameter logistic equation.
